<DocSection Title="Why a mapping abstraction">
    <p>
        A repository spends most of its life translating between two shapes: the
        <b>entity</b> (the row the database stores) and the <b>DTO</b> (the shape
        that crosses the wire). The framework routes all of that through a single
        interface — <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;TEntity, TListDTO, TViewDTO&gt;</code> —
        so <code>ShiftRepository</code> has exactly one mapping path, no matter
        where the mapping itself comes from.
    </p>

    <p>
        One paragraph of history, because older projects and older code comments
        still mention it: AutoMapper was the original implementation, and it has
        been <b>removed from the framework</b>. ShiftEntity, ShiftIdentity and the
        project templates carry no AutoMapper package reference, no AutoMapper
        profile, no <code>AddAutoMapper</code> call and no mapping-mode switch.
        (Consumer applications that have not migrated yet still use AutoMapper in
        their own code — this is a statement about the framework packages, not
        about every codebase that depends on them.) The reason for the change was
        that convention mapping decided at runtime only reported its mistakes at
        runtime, on whichever endpoint a user happened to open first. What replaced
        it decides the same conventions at <b>build time</b>, in a source generator,
        and adds one startup check that no entity/DTO triple is left uncovered.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="The interface">
    <p>The contract is four methods, plus a non-generic marker:</p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"// Non-generic marker: lets a generic parameter be constrained to ""a ShiftEntity mapper""
// without naming the triple (e.g. TMapper on [ShiftEntityEndpointWithMapper<..., TMapper>]).
public interface IShiftEntityMapper { }

public interface IShiftEntityMapper<TEntity, TListDTO, TViewDTO> : IShiftEntityMapper
{
    TViewDTO MapToView(TEntity entity, MappingContext context = default);
    TEntity MapToEntity(TViewDTO dto, TEntity existing, MappingContext context = default);
    IQueryable<TListDTO> MapToList(IQueryable<TEntity> query, MappingContext context = default);
    void CopyEntity(TEntity source, TEntity target, MappingContext context = default);
}")" />

    <ul>
        <li><code>MapToView</code> — translate a single entity into its View+Upsert DTO. Used on get-by-id and after a save.</li>
        <li><code>MapToEntity</code> — merge an incoming DTO <b>into</b> an existing tracked entity and return it. The repository hands you the entity (an empty instance for inserts, the loaded row for updates); the method mutates and returns <code>existing</code> rather than building a detached entity.</li>
        <li><code>MapToList</code> — project an <code>IQueryable&lt;TEntity&gt;</code> into an <code>IQueryable&lt;TListDTO&gt;</code>. It has to stay a translatable member-init expression — no helper calls inside the lambda — so EF Core can turn it into a <code>SELECT</code> and pull only the columns the list DTO needs.</li>
        <li><code>CopyEntity</code> — entity-to-entity copy, used by the <code>ReloadAfterSave</code> path to merge a freshly-loaded entity (navigation properties included) back onto the tracked instance.</li>
        <li><code>MappingContext</code> — carries an <code>IServiceProvider</code> (which may be null) and the action being performed when it is known. The repository passes its <code>DbContext</code>'s application service provider, so a mapper that was never registered in DI — one you <code>new</code> inside <code>UseMapper(...)</code> — can still resolve services on demand instead of constructor-injecting them.</li>
    </ul>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning" Title="A hand-written MapToList must bind ID and IsDeleted">
        <p>
            The Web layer appends <code>.Where(x =&gt; !x.IsDeleted)</code> and
            rewrites hash-id <code>$filter</code> and <code>$orderby</code> against
            the <b>already-projected DTO</b> queryable, not against the entity
            queryable. If your projection never binds those two members, there is
            nothing for any of that to reach.
        </p>
        <p>
            On EF Core an unbound member does not quietly default — the query fails
            to translate and the request throws. A missing <code>IsDeleted</code> is
            a 500 on every list call; a missing <code>ID</code> is a 500 the first
            time anyone sorts or filters, which is the shape that reaches production,
            because the grid works until someone clicks a column header. (In
            LINQ-to-Objects — a unit test over an array — the same omission is
            silent and returns soft-deleted rows instead.)
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>MappingHelpers.MapBaseListFields</code> cannot help here: it is an
            in-memory call and no projection can translate it. Bind the two members
            inline. The source generator binds both automatically — this is a
            contract for hand-written mappers and repository overrides only.
        </p>
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="One mapper per triple, and how the repository finds it">
    <p>
        A mapper is keyed by the <b>triple</b>: entity, list DTO, view/upsert DTO.
        Two endpoints over the same entity with different DTOs are two triples and
        need two mappers — which is why the sample's mapper-backed Country endpoint
        has its own <code>CountryMappedDTO</code> rather than reusing
        <code>CountryDTO</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Resolution happens once, in the repository's constructor
        (<code>InitCommon</code> in <code>ShiftEntity.EFCore/ShiftRepository.cs</code>),
        in this order:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <b>A mapper configured on the repository options</b> —
            <code>UseMapper(mapper)</code> or <code>UseGeneratedMapper(...)</code>.
            That configuration comes either from the builder the repository passes
            to <code>base(db, o =&gt; ...)</code>, or — when no builder is passed —
            from the entity's own
            <code>IConfiguresShiftRepository&lt;E, L, V&gt;.ConfigureRepository</code>.
            Note that <code>UseMapper(null)</code> also counts as "configured" and
            wins over everything below.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>An <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> registered in DI.</b>
            Resolutions that are themselves a <code>ShiftRepository</code> are
            ignored — a repository implements the interface, and using it as its own
            mapper would recurse. This is the path a
            <code>[ShiftEntityEndpointWithMapper&lt;..., TMapper&gt;]</code> attribute
            uses: <code>RegisterShiftRepositories</code> registers the attribute's
            mapper for that triple.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>The source-generated mapper</b> from
            <code>ShiftEntityMapperRegistry</code>, which the generator populates
            from a module initializer.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Nothing</b> — and then the four mapping methods throw
            <code>InvalidOperationException("No mapper configured. Override MapToView() or set one via UseMapper().")</code>.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        There is no convention fallback behind step 4 any more. Sitting above the
        whole chain is one further option: the four methods are
        <code>public virtual</code> on <code>ShiftRepository</code>, so overriding
        <code>MapToView</code> / <code>MapToEntity</code> / <code>MapToList</code> /
        <code>CopyEntity</code> bypasses the resolved mapper entirely unless the
        override calls <code>base</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The repository has a single constructor, and the mapper choice lives in the
        options builder it hands to the base:
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"public class InvoiceRepository
    : ShiftRepository<DB, Invoice, InvoiceListDTO, InvoiceDTO>
{
    public InvoiceRepository(DB db)
        : base(db, options => options.UseGeneratedMapper()) { }
}")" />
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Four ways to supply a mapper">
    <p>
        These are not competing libraries and not a per-project setting. They are
        four levels of explicitness, chosen <b>per repository, in code</b>. Two
        repositories in the same project routinely differ — in the
        <code>StockPlusPlus</code> sample, <code>Product</code> overrides the
        mapping methods outright while <code>Country</code> uses the generated
        mapper with no mapping code at all.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Zero code — the source-generated mapper</h3>
    <p>
        Call <code>options.UseGeneratedMapper()</code>, or configure nothing at all
        and let step 3 of the resolution order find it. The generator discovers the
        triple from your <code>ShiftRepository&lt;DB, E, L, V&gt;</code> subclass or
        from a <code>[ShiftEntityEndpoint...]</code> attribute; no mapper class is
        written by hand anywhere.
    </p>
    <p>
        Sample: <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Repositories/CountryRepository.cs</code>,
        <code>ProductCategoryRepository</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Generated, plus fluent configuration baked at build time</h3>
    <p>
        <code>options.UseGeneratedMapper(map =&gt; map.ForList(...).IgnoreEntity(...))</code>.
        The generator <b>statically reads</b> these calls and emits either the
        customization, the convention, or nothing at all — there is no per-property
        runtime branch. Registering a member automatically suppresses the generated
        convention for it.
    </p>
    <p>The builder surface (<code>ShiftEntity.Core/ShiftMapperBuilder.cs</code>):</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>ForView</code> / <code>ForEntity</code> (plus an <code>existing</code>-aware overload) / <code>ForList</code> (an <b>expression</b> over the entity, spliced into the single SQL projection) / <code>ForCopy</code>.</li>
        <li><code>AfterEntity(dto, existing, context)</code> for work that spans several members.</li>
        <li><code>Ignore</code>, and the per-direction <code>IgnoreView</code> / <code>IgnoreEntity</code> / <code>IgnoreList</code> / <code>IgnoreCopy</code>.</li>
        <li><code>MaxDepth(n)</code> and <code>CaseSensitive()</code>.</li>
        <li>The explicit child composers <code>ForViewChild</code> / <code>ForViewChildren</code>, <code>ForEntityChild</code> / <code>ForEntityChildren</code>, <code>ForListChild</code> / <code>ForListChildren</code>, each taking an optional nested <code>configureChild</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Sample: <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Repositories/InvoiceRepository.cs</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>3. A <code>[ShiftEntityMapper]</code> partial class</h3>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"[ShiftEntityMapper]
public partial class ProductBrandMapper
    : IShiftEntityMapper<ProductBrand, ProductBrandListDTO, ProductBrandDTO>
{
    // Per-property customization. Registering a member suppresses the
    // generated convention for it; everything else stays generated.
    partial void Configure(
        ShiftMapperBuilder<ProductBrand, ProductBrandListDTO, ProductBrandDTO> map)
    {
        map.ForList(d => d.Code, entity => entity.Code ?? ""(No Code)"");
    }

    // Take one method over and still keep every convention: the generated
    // ...Generated body is the partial-class analogue of base.MapToEntity(...).
    public ProductBrand MapToEntity(
        ProductBrandDTO dto, ProductBrand existing, MappingContext context = default)
    {
        existing = MapToEntityGenerated(dto, existing, context);

        existing.Code = existing.Code?.Trim();

        return existing;
    }
}")" />

    <p>
        The generator fills in whichever of the four methods you did not write,
        emits the <code>...Generated</code> bodies you can call, and registers
        <b>this</b> class in the registry instead of an auto-named one. Plug it with
        <code>options.UseMapper(new ProductBrandMapper())</code>, or let the registry
        resolution in step 3 find it.
    </p>
    <p>
        When both exist, precedence is: the mapper's own <code>Configure</code> runs
        first, and the repository's <code>UseGeneratedMapper(configure)</code> runs
        after it and wins.
    </p>
    <p>
        Sample: <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Mappers/ProductBrandMapper.cs</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Hand-written, or repository overrides</h3>
    <p>
        The most explicit option is a plain class implementing
        <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> — sample:
        <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Mappers/CountryMapper.cs</code>, plugged in through a
        <code>[ShiftEntitySecureEndpointWithMapper&lt;CountryMappedDTO, CountryMappedDTO, StockPlusPlusActionTree, CountryMapper&gt;]</code>
        attribute — or overriding the four virtual methods directly on the
        repository, as
        <code>StockPlusPlus.Data/Repositories/ProductRepository.cs</code> does.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning" Title="Override CopyEntity too">
        If you map by hand, remember the fourth method.
        <code>ShiftRepository.CopyEntity</code> no longer falls back to
        <code>ShallowCopyTo</code> when no mapper is configured — it throws like the
        other three. <code>source.ShallowCopyTo(target)</code> is the default body
        to paste.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What the conventions cover">
    <p>
        Everything below is what the generated mapper does for you before you
        configure anything.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <b>Name matching is case-insensitive</b> by default (an exact-case match
            always wins), so bringing across a DTO that a convention mapper used to
            feed does not silently lose members. Opt into exact matching per mapper
            with <code>map.CaseSensitive()</code>. A name that matches several
            members differing only by case, with no exact match, is skipped and
            reported as <code>SHENGEN011</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Scalars</b>, symmetrically in both directions: an implicit conversion
            is used as-is; <code>T?</code> into <code>T</code> becomes
            <code>?? default</code>; conversion to and from <code>string</code> for
            every numeric type, <code>bool</code>, <code>DateTime</code>,
            <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, <code>TimeSpan</code>, <code>DateOnly</code>,
            <code>TimeOnly</code>, <code>Guid</code> and enums (enum text parses by
            name or number, case-insensitively); numeric to numeric and enum to
            numeric via an explicit cast. Two unrelated enums are <b>not</b>
            converted — that is a mistake, not a conversion.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Text is always formatted and parsed with the invariant culture</b>,
            because a DTO value crosses machines and locales. Malformed input throws
            <code>ShiftEntityMappingException</code>, which names the member and the
            value, and is its own exception type so a host can treat it as bad input
            (400) rather than a server fault (500). It deliberately does not
            parse-or-default: a member that quietly becomes <code>0</code> or
            <code>1970-01-01</code> saves a row that looks fine and is wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Foreign keys.</b> An entity <code>XID</code> of type <code>long</code>
            or <code>long?</code> pairs with a DTO member <code>X</code> of type
            <code>ShiftEntitySelectDTO</code>, taking the display text from the
            navigation's text property when there is one. The write direction
            converts back. The suffix test ignores case, so <code>CompanyId</code>
            works too.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Files.</b> A <code>List&lt;ShiftFileDTO&gt;</code> on the DTO pairs
            with a <code>string</code> JSON column on the entity.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Base fields.</b> For a <code>ShiftEntityViewAndUpsertDTO</code> over a
            <code>ShiftEntity</code>, <code>MapBaseFields</code> fills
            <code>ID</code>, <code>IsDeleted</code> and the four audit fields; in the
            list projection <code>ID</code> and <code>IsDeleted</code> are bound
            inline. <code>Tags</code> and <code>Revisions</code> are deliberately
            left alone — the repository splices tags in on the read side, so mappers
            never touch them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Collections.</b> Element type and container type are converted
            independently — <code>List</code>, <code>ICollection</code>,
            <code>IEnumerable</code>, the <code>IReadOnly</code> interfaces,
            <code>HashSet</code> and arrays in any pairing, for simple and complex
            elements alike, materialised into the member's own container type.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Deep composition</h3>
    <p>
        A "child" is any pairable class — different from the entity class, not
        abstract, not itself an entity type, and not
        <code>ShiftEntitySelectDTO</code> or <code>ShiftFileDTO</code> — as a single
        object or as a collection. That includes plain JSON-column POCOs and owned
        types, not just <code>ShiftEntity</code> navigations. Children are composed
        <b>automatically</b> up to a maximum depth (10 by default; set it per
        repository, mapper or assembly with
        <code>[ShiftEntityMapperMaxDepth(n)]</code>, or fluently with
        <code>map.MaxDepth(n)</code>):
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>View</b> — the child DTO is built through the generated pair mapper.</li>
        <li><b>Entity (write)</b> — replace-with-new: each child DTO becomes a brand-new child entity. This is not a merge and not an update-by-ID.</li>
        <li><b>List</b> — composed inline into the SQL projection as a correlated member-init, recursively, so EF turns it into joins.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        All three directions compose the <b>same</b> set of members — a child the
        view reads is a child the entity writes — because that asymmetry is exactly
        the bug this design exists to prevent (children that read back fine and are
        silently emptied on save). The explicit
        <code>ForViewChild</code> / <code>ForEntityChild</code> / <code>ForListChild</code>
        family (and the <code>...Children</code> forms) is for customizing a
        particular child or for composing <i>past</i> the cap. A cycle is detected,
        the edge skipped, and <code>SHENGEN003</code> raised.
    </p>

    <h3>What the conventions deliberately do not do</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <b>The list direction is restricted to what EF can turn into SQL</b>:
            casts, and <code>ToString()</code> on numerics only. Parsing text has no
            trustworthy SQL equivalent, and casting a date or a bool to text is
            provider-specific, so those conversions are not emitted into the
            projection — the member is reported by <code>SHENGEN007</code> at build
            time instead of failing at query time.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Flattening is not implemented, on purpose.</b>
            <code>Vehicle.VIN</code> does not become <code>VehicleVIN</code>. An
            unbound member is never silently invented; it is reported.
            <code>SHENGEN007</code> even prints a paste-ready
            <code>map.ForList(...)</code> line with the flattened path filled in when
            the name resolves to one, which is what makes leaving flattening out
            affordable.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Init-only DTO properties</b> cannot be assigned by a generated mapper
            (it constructs and then sets), so they are reported as unmapped, with the
            fix being to give the property a settable setter.
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Dropping a member</b> is explicit:
            <code>[ShiftEntityMapperIgnore]</code> on the property, or
            <code>map.Ignore(...)</code> and its per-direction siblings. The member is
            <i>omitted from the generated code</i> rather than skipped at runtime, and
            a complex child's whole subtree is pruned with it.
        </li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What the build tells you: the SHENGEN diagnostics">
    <p>
        The split is deliberate. <b>Errors</b> mark something that cannot be
        expressed at build time, or that would run silently wrong.
        <b>Warnings</b> mark something merely skipped — promote any of them with
        <code>&lt;WarningsAsErrors&gt;SHENGEN007&lt;/WarningsAsErrors&gt;</code>.
        All of them are raised by
        <code>ShiftEntity.SourceGenerator/ShiftEntityMapperGenerator.cs</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>SHENGEN001</code> <b>(error)</b> — a <code>[ShiftEntityMapper]</code> class is not <code>partial</code>. Make it partial.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN002</code> <b>(error)</b> — a <code>[ShiftEntityMapper]</code> class implements neither <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> nor <code>IShiftObjectMapper&lt;E, D&gt;</code>. Add the interface.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN003</code> <b>(warning)</b> — a deep-mapping cycle; the member is skipped. Customize it, or take it off the DTO.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN004</code> <b>(warning)</b> — view DTO members that no convention and no deep composition reaches. Map them with <code>ForView</code> or <code>Configure</code>, take the method over, or adjust the DTO.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN005</code> <b>(error)</b> — mapper configuration registered inside an <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>switch</code>, loop, <code>?:</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code> or <code>??</code>. Decisions are baked, so a skipped branch would silently drop the member to its default. Register unconditionally and put the condition <i>inside</i> the value delegate — <code>map.ForView(d =&gt; d.X, (e, _) =&gt; cond ? a : b)</code>. There is deliberately no runtime opt-out.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN006</code> <b>(error)</b> — an entity declares <code>IConfiguresShiftRepository&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> while a repository for the same triple passes an options builder to its base constructor. Passing a builder means the repository takes over completely, so the entity's <code>ConfigureRepository</code> would never run. Move the configuration into the repository's builder, or drop the builder.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN007</code> <b>(warning)</b> — list DTO members the projection does not bind, so the column comes back empty. The message prints a paste-ready <code>map.ForList(...)</code> line, filling in the flattened path when the name resolves to one.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN008</code> <b>(warning)</b> — DTO members that <code>MapToView</code> reads and <code>MapToEntity</code> never writes back: they display correctly and silently fail to save. Map them with <code>ForEntity</code>, or declare them read-only with <code>IgnoreEntity</code>. This is deliberately the read/write <i>asymmetry</i>, not a mirror of 004, which would warn on every internal column.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN009</code> <b>(error)</b> — configuration the generator cannot read at build time: a selector that is not a plain property access, a non-constant depth, an open-generic receiver, and above all a registration made from <b>another assembly</b>. Without this the generator would bake the plain convention and the call would do nothing at all — <code>map.Ignore(x =&gt; x.Secret)</code> would compile, run, and still map the member. Its runtime twin is <code>ShiftMapperBuilder.VerifyBaked</code>, which throws at first use for the cross-assembly case no compilation-local analysis can see.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN010</code> <b>(warning)</b> — automatic deep <i>write</i> would replace tracked child rows that carry a required foreign key back to the parent, so saving would either fail on the FK or orphan and duplicate rows. Reconcile instead with <code>map.AfterEntity(...)</code> or the <code>existing</code>-aware <code>map.ForEntity(...)</code>, or <code>IgnoreEntity</code> the member and handle it in the repository.</li>
        <li><code>SHENGEN011</code> <b>(warning)</b> — a name matches several members differing only by case, and none exactly; the member is skipped. Rename one, or map it explicitly.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Helpers for hand-written mappers">
    <p>
        Hand-writing a mapper gets tedious fast for entities with audit fields,
        foreign keys and file references, so <code>ShiftEntity.Core</code> ships a
        set of extension helpers (<code>MappingHelpers</code>) that take the
        boilerplate away while keeping the mapping explicit. Generated mappers emit
        calls into these same helpers, so the two styles produce identical values.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>dto.MapBaseFields(entity)</code> — <code>ID</code>, <code>IsDeleted</code> and the four audit fields onto a view/upsert DTO. Returns the DTO for chaining.</li>
        <li><code>dto.MapBaseListFields(entity)</code> — <code>ID</code> and <code>IsDeleted</code> onto a list DTO. <b>In-memory only</b>: never usable inside an <code>IQueryable</code> projection.</li>
        <li><code>source.CopyBaseFields(target)</code> — audit fields plus <code>IsDeleted</code>, entity to entity.</li>
        <li><code>source.ShallowCopyTo(target)</code> — cached-reflection shallow copy of every settable property <i>except</i> <code>ID</code>, <code>ReloadAfterSave</code> and <code>AuditFieldsAreSet</code>. This is the default body for a hand-written <code>CopyEntity</code>.</li>
        <li><code>id.ToSelectDTO(text)</code> for <code>long</code> and <code>long?</code>, and <code>selectDTO.ToForeignKey()</code> / <code>.ToNullableForeignKey()</code> back the other way.</li>
        <li><code>json.ToShiftFiles()</code> / <code>files.ToJsonString()</code> — between a JSON column and a <code>List&lt;ShiftFileDTO&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li>Scalars: <code>ToLong</code> / <code>ToNullableLong</code>, <code>ToGuid</code> / <code>ToNullableGuid</code>, <code>ToInvariantText</code>, <code>ToValue&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ToNullableValue&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ToEnum&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ToNullableEnum&lt;T&gt;</code> — invariant culture throughout, throwing <code>ShiftEntityMappingException</code> (naming the member, the value and the target type) rather than defaulting.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>With these helpers, a repository that maps a Product by hand looks like:</p>

    <DocCodeBlock Code="@(@"public override ProductDTO MapToView(Product entity, MappingContext context = default) =>
    new ProductDTO
    {
        Name = entity.Name,
        Price = entity.Price,

        // Foreign key + display label in one call
        ProductCategory = entity.ProductCategoryID.ToSelectDTO(entity.ProductCategory?.Name),
        ProductBrand = entity.ProductBrandID.ToSelectDTO(entity.ProductBrand?.Name),
    }
    .MapBaseFields(entity);   // ID, IsDeleted + audit fields, fluent")" />

    <p>
        The result is mapping code that is still explicit — every field is visible —
        without drowning in <code>CreateDate = entity.CreateDate</code> repetitions.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="When nothing covers a triple the app does not start">
    <p>
        <code>RegisterShiftRepositories(...)</code> wires
        <code>ShiftEntityMapperValidation.Validate</code> unconditionally — there is
        no mode or flag that turns it off. The check is type-level, so it runs
        without booting the app.
    </p>
    <p>
        It walks every triple it can discover (endpoint attributes plus
        <code>ShiftRepository</code> subclasses) and counts one as covered if any of
        the following holds: a DI registration for
        <code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code> exists, the registry holds a
        generated mapper for it, or the repository itself <i>declares</i> one of the
        four mapping methods. That last test is deliberately based on the declaring
        type — asking "does this type have a <code>MapToView</code>?" is true of
        every repository and would validate nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
        Everything it finds is reported together, in one
        <code>InvalidOperationException</code>: uncovered triples (<i>"(Product,
        ProductListDTO, ProductDTO) — no mapper. Add a [ShiftEntityMapper] partial
        class, call UseMapper/UseGeneratedMapper in the repository, or override the
        mapping methods."</i>), registry conflicts, and mappers that can no longer
        bind to this version of ShiftEntity — a generated mapper is code frozen on
        its own build day, so the fix there is to rebuild and republish the assembly
        that owns it.
    </p>
    <p>
        In one line: this is the difference between caught in CI and caught in
        production. The same gap used to be a 500 on whichever endpoint a user
        opened first.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note">
        If a repository is nonetheless constructed with no mapper — for example
        <code>options.UseMapper(null)</code> and no override — each of the four
        mapping methods throws <i>"No mapper configured. Override MapToView() or set
        one via UseMapper()."</i> on use.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Where the generator runs">
    <p>
        <code>ShiftEntity.SourceGenerator</code> ships as an <b>analyzer</b> inside
        the <code>ShiftSoftware.ShiftEntity</code> package, so it runs on the project
        that declares your entities and repositories. In the sample that is
        <code>StockPlusPlus.Data</code> — look for generated mappers there, not in
        the test projects.
    </p>
    <p>
        It emits triple mappers (<code>IShiftEntityMapper&lt;E, L, V&gt;</code>) plus
        <b>pair</b> mappers (<code>IShiftObjectMapper&lt;childEntity, childDto&gt;</code>)
        for child members, recursively, and registers them into
        <code>ShiftEntityMapperRegistry</code> from a module initializer.
        Reflection-only scans must force module constructors first; the framework
        does this for you.
    </p>
    <p>
        On attribute-driven endpoints:
        <code>[ShiftEntityEndpoint&lt;TList, TView&gt;]</code> and its secure form
        keep the built-in repository, which resolves the generated mapper on its own.
        Setting <code>UseGeneratedMapper = true</code> makes that explicit and fails
        loudly if no generated mapper exists for the triple (it is rejected alongside
        a custom repository or a mapper attribute).
        <code>[ShiftEntityEndpointWithMapper&lt;TList, TView, TMapper&gt;]</code> and
        <code>[ShiftEntitySecureEndpointWithMapper&lt;...&gt;]</code> keep the built-in
        repository but supply the mapper; <code>TMapper</code> is constrained to the
        non-generic <code>IShiftEntityMapper</code> marker and the exact triple is
        validated at discovery. A mapper-backed endpoint needs its <b>own</b> DTO
        type, because mappers are keyed by DTO type.
    </p>
</DocSection>

